        "position": 7,
        "title": "Adjusts for a great fit",
        "text": "I got this as a way to carry a lazy or injured dog ( mine or one of the pups I foster) I have had a very difficult time getting my 20 lb dog into a sport backpack I got for hiking, she is very afraid of not having her feet on the ground. I thought maybe this would help her conquer her fear because it supports differently than a regular pack.I could tell this would end in success right away, she willingly stepped into the front leg holes, and gave me no resistance with her back legs. It has many straps to secure the pet, and uses different color clasps to make it easy to identify which strap goes where. There are 4 that strap from one side to the other, keeping it on, and they are very adjustable for a snug fit(which is very important). It also has a strap on each side that runs from the strap the human wears across the dog and attaches, this keeps the dog from wobbling up and down, and made my fearful dog actually enjoy her 'ride'. The human portion is a simple strap going from shoulder to shoulder that attaches to the dog carrier, and one strap over each shoulder. it also has a strap that goes across the chest for extra security, but it is not made for a large chested person. It is very lightweight and doesn't make you overheat when using on a hot day. The dog simply hangs there in a sling style feet down. The interesting thing was how calm and relaxed my pup was the whole time she was riding on my back. She kept her front paw sitting on my hip and her head leaned against my back. She seemed comfortable and didn't squirm while in it, also I noticed that instead of jumping out and running off like has happened with every other carrying item I have tried with her. I don't think that this is meant for long distance use, there is no padding anywhere on it, this is a bare bones model, something to keep in mind. My shoulders were quite sore the next morning. This would be a 5 star item if it had more padding around the leg holes in the sling. I think this is necessary, a few hours after our trial run, I noticed she was walking stiffly and when I was massaging her legs, she whimpered when I got to the part that was holding her weight. It seems well made with strong nylon straps, and I was not worried it would break and my dog would fall at any time.",
